/// Which insured item a reported damage concerns, or why the claim is refused.
/// A damage names only an item type, so the office looks the type up in the
/// policy; the item it finds is what the reimbursement clauses are then judged
/// against, since they read the insured item's own enchantment and material
/// rather than anything in the report. A type the policy does not cover is
/// refused here: the office will not settle a damage to an item it never
/// insured, and it names the offending type in its refusal.
///
/// Resolving the damage and refusing it are one decision, not two. The office
/// has a further ruling pending on a damage it cannot match -- more damages of
/// a type than the policy insures -- and that ruling lands on both halves at
/// once: it changes how a damage is tied to an insured item (each insured item
/// answering for at most one damage, rather than the first of its type
/// answering for all), and the refusal it adds is one only the matching itself
/// can detect. A lookup that merely reported "no such item" would have thrown
/// away what that refusal needs to say, so the match and its refusal stay
